{"causes":["Development of the first medical schools in ancient Greece and Rome","Establishment of the Academy of Athens, where physicians such as Hippocrates and Galen studied and taught","Observations of the human body and its functions by early physicians, leading to the development of the four humors theory"],"effects":["Establishment of the first comprehensive textbooks of human anatomy, such as Andreas Vesalius' 'De humani corporis fabrica'","Discovery of the circulatory system by William Harvey, leading to a greater understanding of blood and its role in transmitting diseases","Development of modern surgical techniques and treatments for conditions such as malaria and tuberculosis"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Hippocrates","sourceName":"Hippocrates","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Physiology","targetName":"Physiology","context":"Hippocrates' observations of the human body and its functions laid the groundwork for the development of physiology as a scientific discipline"},{"sourceSlug":"Andreas Vesalius","sourceName":"Andreas Vesalius","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Physiology","targetName":"Physiology","context":"Vesalius' 'De humani corporis fabrica' became a foundational textbook of human anatomy and helped establish physiology as a respected scientific field"},{"sourceSlug":"William Harvey","sourceName":"William Harvey","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Physiology","targetName":"Physiology","context":"Harvey's discovery of the circulatory system led to a greater understanding of blood and its role in transmitting diseases, informing treatments and therapies that improve lives worldwide"},{"sourceSlug":"Galen","sourceName":"Galen","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Physiology","targetName":"Physiology","context":"Galen's work built upon the discoveries of earlier physicians, contributing to the development of physiology as a scientific discipline"},{"sourceSlug":"Academy of Athens","sourceName":"Academy of Athens","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Physiology","targetName":"Physiology","context":"The Academy of Athens provided a platform for physicians such as Hippocrates and Galen to share their knowledge and advance the field of physiology"},{"sourceSlug":"Plato","sourceName":"Plato","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Physiology","targetName":"Physiology","context":"Plato's philosophical ideas about the human body and its functions influenced the development of physiology as a scientific discipline"},{"sourceSlug":"Mary Shelley","sourceName":"Mary Shelley","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Physiology","targetName":"Physiology","context":"Shelley's novel 'Frankenstein' reflects the growing interest in human anatomy and the natural world, influencing the development of physiology as a scientific field"},{"sourceSlug":"American Physiological Society","sourceName":"American Physiological Society","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Physiology","targetName":"Physiology","context":"The American Physiological Society continues to promote the advancement of physiology and its applications in medicine and science"}],"places":[{"name":"Greece","role":"birthplace"},{"name":"Rome","role":"site of early medical schools"},{"name":"Athens","role":"site of the Academy of Athens"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: physiology","type":"Reference article","slug":"physiology"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q521","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physiology"],"tags":["methodology","Political & Polemical Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"physiology left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 133 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Physiology (from Greek φύσις, physis, 'nature', and λόγος, logos, 'study') is the branch of biology concerned with the normal functions and mechanisms of living organisms — the study of how cells, tissues, organs, and organ systems work together to maintain life, respond to the environment, and support vital processes including metabolism, respiration, circulation, digestion, neural signalling, reproduction, and homeostasis. Physiology is both an ancient and a modern discipline — its roots extend to Hippocratic and Galenic medicine (the humoral theory of physiological function that dominated Western medicine from antiquity to the 17th century) and its modern scientific form was established in the 17th–19th centuries through the work of William Harvey (discovery of blood circulation, 1628), René Descartes (mechanical model of the body), Albrecht von Haller (irritability and sensibility of tissues, 1750s), Claude Bernard (internal environment and homeostasis, 1850s–1860s), and the development of experimental physiology as an autonomous discipline.

Modern physiology is subdivided into numerous specialisms: cellular and molecular physiology (the function of individual cells and their molecular mechanisms), systems physiology (the function of organ systems — cardiovascular, respiratory, renal, gastrointestinal, neurological, endocrine, reproductive), comparative physiology (comparing physiological mechanisms across different organisms), exercise physiology (the physiological responses to physical activity), and pathophysiology (the physiological mechanisms of disease). The boundaries between physiology and related disciplines — biochemistry, molecular biology, pharmacology, neuroscience, and medicine — are fluid and overlapping, reflecting physiology's position as the integrative discipline that links molecular mechanisms to whole-organism function.

The history of physiology is inseparable from the history of medicine — the understanding of how the body works in health is the foundation for understanding how it fails in disease, and physiological research has directly generated most of the major advances in clinical medicine: Harvey's circulation underpinning cardiovascular medicine, Bernard's homeostasis concept underpinning metabolic medicine and intensive care, and the discoveries of electrophysiology underpinning cardiology and neurology. The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine has been awarded annually since 1901 and has recognised the most significant physiological discoveries of the 20th and 21st centuries.","subjects":["Biology","Ancient Era","Medicine","Science","Homeostasis","Experimental Science","History of Medicine","Academic Discipline"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Physiology, the scientific study of functions and mechanisms in living systems, has had a profound impact on our understanding of human health and disease, informing treatments and therapies that improve lives worldwide.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
